i agree with janis.  "You read the scroll of treasure map."  "It says, 
'To find my treasure, get thee to a place where an X scars the floor.'  
'Thence, ye may travel [north] [22] paces, and [east] [6] paces.'  'Dig 
down, and ye shall find my precious booty.'" (hehehe... "booty")

ideas:

generate a random object, and use that as the origin ("First, find my 
blade... ye shall know it, for it's curved like the crescent moon.").  
perhaps make the object cursed, to discourage creatures from moving it 
about.  (this would require a list of "piratized" descriptions of many 
different items)

or... a grave as the origin, ("Find the last resting place of me mate, 
Blind Tom.  From there, go...")

or... the "X" should be written (not burned or engraved) in the dust... 
if you don't find it quickly, it may get wiped out by monsters or pets.

if the point-of-origin marker gets moved or obliterated, you may have to 
resort to object detection (not a tremendous inconvenience), or simply 
leave the treasure behind.  this would add a bit of pressure to find the 
spot quickly.

to compensate for all this trouble, the pirate's booty should be pretty 
good... say, a guarantee of at least one valuable gem, and a good amount 
of gold (proportional to the dungeon level, of course)

i am still attracted to the idea of a small puzzle instead of simply 
"ten paces north, seventeen paces east", though... what sort of puzzles 
could give a numerical answer?  (besides artithmetic problems... this is 
nethack, not Number Munchers)

"if ye have ten apples, and i take six, how many apples do i have?"

